[Slide 1 – Introduction]

Good morning everyone.

Today, I am recommending a tactical short position in USDTHB, meaning long the Thai baht against the US dollar, with a one-to-two-month investment horizon.

The core thesis is that several supportive factors are beginning to align at the same time: Thailand’s external balance is moving into a more favorable seasonal period, tourism inflows are recovering, and the broad US dollar backdrop is becoming less supportive.

Seasonality also suggests that July and August have historically offered attractive entry windows for this trade.

I will first discuss the Thai baht fundamentals, then the US dollar backdrop, before moving to timing and execution.

[Slide 2 – THB fundamentals]

Starting with the Thai side, the domestic picture is becoming more supportive for the baht.

First, Thailand’s external balance typically improves after the weaker second quarter. As the chart shows, the current account has historically strengthened in Q3, partly because tourism receipts and other seasonal FX inflows begin to recover.

Second, that tourism recovery is already visible in the high-frequency data. Weekly foreign arrivals rose from below 500,000 in mid-June to around 580,000 by late July.

This matters because tourism is effectively a service export for Thailand. More foreign visitors generate additional foreign-currency receipts, increasing the supply of dollars that is converted into baht.

Finally, the Bank of Thailand appears increasingly sensitive to renewed THB weakness. Changes in FX reserves are consistent with greater dollar selling by the central bank, although reserve data alone cannot prove intervention.

Taken together, an improving external balance, recovering tourism and greater policy sensitivity should provide a stronger fundamental floor for the baht.

[Slide 3 – USD backdrop]

The second part of the thesis is that US dollar support is beginning to weaken.

The July FOMC did not deliver the rate hike that some investors had feared. However, the front end of the US rates curve still appears to price a meaningful risk of renewed tightening.

In my view, that pricing is too hawkish. Wage growth is moderating, seasonal inflation pressures should fade, and the threshold for another Fed hike remains high.

If markets gradually remove this additional tightening premium, US front-end yields should fall and reduce the dollar’s rate advantage.

Positioning reinforces this asymmetry. CFTC data suggest that speculative long-dollar positioning remains relatively crowded. Therefore, even a modest dovish repricing could generate a larger position unwind.

Recent US–Japan coordination also suggests that policymakers are becoming less tolerant of excessive dollar strength.

Overall, I expect the dollar to consolidate, with the balance of risks increasingly skewed toward the downside.

[Slide 4 – Broader market backdrop]

This matters directly for USDTHB because the currency pair has remained sensitive to broad dollar movements.

The left-hand chart shows that USDTHB and DXY have generally moved in the same direction over the recent cycle. Therefore, if the broad dollar weakens, USDTHB should move lower and the baht should appreciate.

Gold provides an additional, but secondary, source of support.

Historically, higher gold prices have often coincided with a stronger baht. Part of this reflects Thailand’s gold-related FX flows, while both gold and THB can also benefit from a weaker-dollar environment.

I would not treat gold as the primary driver of the trade. However, an upside break in gold could reinforce the broader short-USDTHB thesis.

[Slide 5 – Seasonality]

The fundamental view is also supported by seasonality.

This backtest shows the performance of a one-month USDTHB position based on different entry dates over the past ten years. Red represents profitable short-USDTHB entries, while green represents profitable long entries.

July and August show a relatively clear concentration of profitable short entries.

Seasonality is not a standalone trading signal, but it provides useful quantitative support for entering the position now, when the fundamental backdrop is also improving.

[Slide 6 – Execution and risks]

For execution, I recommend shorting USDTHB spot at approximately 33.2, with a target of 32.0 and a stop-loss at 33.9.

This gives us around 1.2 baht of potential upside against 0.7 baht of downside, producing a reward-to-risk ratio of approximately 1.7 before funding and carry.

I prefer spot because it provides clean and transparent exposure to the underlying FX move and allows us to exit flexibly if the thesis changes.

However, the trade has negative carry because we are long the lower-yielding baht against the higher-yielding dollar. This is why I would keep the position tactical, with a one-to-two-month horizon, rather than holding it indefinitely.

The main risks are a renewed hawkish repricing of the Fed, a sharp rise in oil prices or escalation in the Middle East, and a disappointment in tourism or current-account inflows.

The stop at 33.9 protects us if these risks begin to dominate.

To conclude, this is a tactical trade based on alignment: improving Thai FX inflows, fading US dollar support and favorable seasonal timing. Together, these factors support a move in USDTHB from 33.2 toward 32.0 over the next one to two months.
